{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_001","claim":"The target overfits the nocturnal UHI gap to a single cross-city modulator (urban-canyon geometry / sky view factor), despite explicitly acknowledging in its own counterconditions that anthropogenic heat release or latent-heat deficit can dominate the energy balance, which contradicts its claim that a single mechanism governs cross-city variance.","grounds":"The problem asks what 'single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others.' The target's claim asserts that cross-city variance is 'governed by the geometry of the urban canyon,' and its mechanism paragraph attributes the larger nocturnal warm anomaly specifically to 'a larger, denser city with deeper canyons and weaker ventilation.' However, the target's counterconditions concede that 'If anthropogenic heat release or latent-heat deficit dominates the energy balance, geometry alone cannot explain the largest nocturnal gaps.' This shows the target recognizes circumstances in which its stated single mechanism is insufficient to account for the largest nocturnal gaps, undermining its answer to the problem. The target also lists anthropogenic heat and latent heat in its mechanism paragraph but relegates them to secondary roles, even though its own countercondition admits they can dominate—creating an internal tension that invalidates its 'governed by' framing for cross-city variance.","premise":"The target's claim asserts cross-city variance is governed by urban-canyon geometry, but its countercondition admits anthropogenic heat or latent-heat deficit can dominate and render geometry alone insufficient.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"b89848282fdd469cd15ab22d3ff3ebe5e3f2aa663d9a39e6caf38840572642db","quote":"The nocturnal UHI and its cross-city variance are governed by the geometry of the urban canyon: deep, dense building arrays shrink the sky view factor, trapping longwave radiation and slowing radiative cooling, so cities with greater built density and taller street walls retain more daytime stored heat after sunset and cool more slowly."},{"block":"b89848282fdd469cd15ab22d3ff3ebe5e3f2aa663d9a39e6caf38840572642db","quote":"If anthropogenic heat release or latent-heat deficit dominates the energy balance, geometry alone cannot explain the largest nocturnal gaps."}],"cited_input_aliases":["SRC_001"]}